	<div class="page-content">
		<h2>3) Next Steps for Learned Query Optimization
			<p><a href="https://rmarcus.info/blog/" target="_blank">Ryan Marcus</a>
				&nbsp; <small>University of Pennsylvania</small></p>
		</h2>
		<h2>Abstract</h2>
		<p>
			Since 2018, the database community has found a myriad of ways to use reinforcement learning (RL) for query planning. While scientifically interesting and an important step forward, these techniques (including my own) failed to see much adoption in industry due to their sample inefficiency, unpredictable behavior, and added critical path complexity. Our lab's recent work has circumvented this problem in two ways. First, we show that one can pull RL "up," using RL to optimize an entire query workload offline, and then using only verified query plans at runtime. Second, we show that one can push RL "down" into the execution engine itself, where each morsel becomes a sample and incorrect decisions are "learned away" during the execution of a single query. We believe both of these directions are promising, and show some preliminary evidence from industry for the first proposal. 
</p>

		<h2>Bio</h2>
		<p style="float: left;"> <img src="./images/ryan_marcus.avif" height="auto" width="120px" border="1px"> </p>
		<p>
			Ryan Marcus is an assistant professor at the University of Pennsylvania, where he focuses on using machine learning to build the next generation of data management tools that automatically adapt to new hardware and user workloads, invent novel processing strategies, and understand user intention. Prior to Penn, Ryan was a postdoc at MIT (supervised by Tim Kraska), and received his PhD from Brandeis University (advised by Olga Papaemmanouil). Ryan has also held research positions at Amazon, Intel Labs, and the Los Alamos National Laboratory. 
		</p>
	
